```java
// A hand-rolled sort that bakes the comparison rule into the algorithm.
// To sort by a different field you must copy this whole method and edit it.
static void sortByPrice(List<Product> items) {
    for (int i = 0; i < items.size(); i++) {
        for (int j = 0; j < items.size() - 1; j++) {
            if (items.get(j).price() > items.get(j + 1).price()) { // rule is fixed
                var tmp = items.get(j);
                items.set(j, items.get(j + 1));
                items.set(j + 1, tmp);
            }
        }
    }
}
// sortByName(), sortByStock()... each duplicates the entire algorithm.
```

```java
record Product(String name, double price, int stock) {}

// Each Comparator is a separate strategy for the SAME sort method.
Comparator<Product> byPrice = Comparator.comparingDouble(Product::price);
Comparator<Product> byStockDesc = Comparator.comparingInt(Product::stock).reversed();
Comparator<Product> byName = Comparator.comparing(Product::name);

var products = new ArrayList<>(List.of(
    new Product("Laptop", 1200.0, 5),
    new Product("Mouse", 25.0, 150),
    new Product("Keyboard", 75.0, 30)));

products.sort(byPrice);      // same sort method, strategy 1
products.sort(byStockDesc);  // strategy 2 at runtime
products.sort(byName);        // strategy 3

// A lambda IS an anonymous strategy:
products.sort((a, b) -> Double.compare(a.price(), b.price()));
// Compose strategies: by name, then by price when names tie
products.sort(byName.thenComparing(byPrice));
```

*The sort method never changes — only the injected `Comparator` does. That is Strategy.*

